From ca41cf546f556ec6c623e21f8374606925ef6ecf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:04:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] deflake cron delivery tests: wait for the loop
 thread to be running before delivering

test_partial_split_delivery_is_not_resent_by_standalone flaked on CI (run 43498034019, job
132136563277): "Expected send to have been awaited once. Awaited 0 times."

Why: the test started `loop.run_forever` on a daemon thread and called `_deliver_result` at once.
`_prepare_target_delivery` takes the live-adapter lane only when `loop.is_running()` is already
True; on a CPU-throttled runner the loop thread had not reached run_forever yet, so delivery went
down the standalone lane and the live adapter was never awaited. The two sibling tests in
TestDeliverResultTimeoutCancelsFuture start their loop the same way (the in-flight one fails the same
way under the same delay).

Fix (test-only): `_start_running_loop()` schedules an Event-setting callback before starting the
thread and waits (30 s hang bound, returns at once normally) until run_forever has run it, i.e.
after is_running() flips. All three tests use it. Both live-loop classes
(TestDeliverResultTimeoutCancelsFuture, TestDeliverResultPartialSplitDelivery) move unchanged otherwise
to tests/cron/test_scheduler_live_loop_delivery.py to keep test_scheduler.py under its size ratchet.

Proof: a pytest plugin that sleeps 0.2 s at the top of BaseEventLoop.run_forever (the descheduled
loop thread) reproduces the exact CI assertion on the old tests (2 failed, 1 passed) and passes
3/3 with the fix; tests/cron/test_scheduler.py + the new file 90 passed; ruff clean.

